  "id": "Compound::DB00649 - Gene::9356",
  "verbalization": "Stavudine, a nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor, is linked to Slc22a6 through multiple pathways that converge on its inclusion in the class of nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors. This class includes Didanosine and Zidovudine, both of which bind to Slc22a6. The shared lowest common ancestor of Stavudine, Didanosine, and Zidovudine is the N-Glycosyl Compound, reinforcing the structural and functional relationship between these entities. The evidence suggests that Stavudine, as part of this class, interacts with Slc22a6 through a common mechanism of binding, which is consistent with the observed interactions of its analogs. The relationship is further supported by the causal link between Stavudine and Symptomatic Hyperlactatemia, which is mediated by Didanosine, both of which bind to Slc22a6. The integrated evidence highlights a mechanistic pathway where Stavudine, through its class membership and binding to Slc22a6, contributes to the pathophysiology associated with its use. The consistency across different pathways strengthens the hypothesis that Stavudine's interaction with Slc22a6 is a biologically relevant and mechanistically sound process.",
